Z||000000||Jake2m4||06-23-2001||08:50 PM||||Will a 3800 from a camaro work as easily as one from a front wheel drive car?  Using the fiero manual trans.  I understand that the '98 is a 3800 series II, like the ones in buicks and others.  The reason I'm asking is b/c most of the info I've found only talks about front wheel drive uses of the 3800.<P>Jake<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||06-24-2001||12:45 AM||||I'm not sure of any other similarities/dfferences, but the biggest problem is the starter is on the wrong side  for the Fiero.  You'd have to redrill them on the opposite side of the block (ala 3.4 conversions).  <P><BR>Other than that, I'm not sure why it wouldn't work.  Of course you don't have the option of a supercharger,  like on  the Bonneville SSEi or Buick Park Ave. Ultra, but the NA 3800 from a Camaro/Firebird should work (theoretically with the starter holes re-drilled).<P>Maybe someone else can elaborate?  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake2m4||06-24-2001||06:14 PM||||Thanks mrfiero, I knew that about the 3.4 but is it the same with a 3800?  Could some one give me a little more info?<P>I'm not so worried about the supercharger, b/c my friend wrecked his camaro (I was in the passenger seat at the time, and no one was hurt), and I can probably get a good deal from him if the insurance company totals it.  We're not sure what they'll do, it could be fixed but would need thousands in repairs.<P><BR>Jake<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
